# scripts/deploy.sh settings, so a routine install is `./scripts/deploy.sh`
# with no flags.
#
# This file is SOURCED, not parsed — it can compute its values, and it runs as
# you. deploy.sh refuses to read it if it is group- or world-writable.
#
#   ./scripts/deploy.sh --init-config    # writes this file at 0600 and names it
#
# The directory is named after this agent rather than after the project, so
# every agent in the fleet carries its own. That is what keeps their owner
# keys apart: an agent's on-chain id derives from its key, so two agents
# sharing one would be a single id claiming two cards.
#
# Precedence: flag > environment > config file > default. So
# `./scripts/deploy.sh --public-url https://staging.example.org` still works,
# and so does a one-off `SVPCHAIN_DEPLOY_HOST=other ./scripts/deploy.sh`.

# ---- the host and the chain ------------------------------------------------

# SSH target. Needs docker and compose v2 usable without sudo.
SVPCHAIN_DEPLOY_HOST="www@svpdev1.example.com"

# Optional SSH jump host (ssh -J) when the deploy host is not reachable from
# this machine directly. Comma-separate to chain several hops.
#SVPCHAIN_DEPLOY_JUMP_BOX="ops@bastion.example.com"

# The chain carrying x/agent — the agent registry. Used by --register only, and
# the registration signature commits to it. The agent itself never dials a
# chain: every operation it serves is a call to the MCP server below.
#
# Formerly SVPCHAIN_CHAIN_ID, back when it also named the DEX chain the agent
# served. That second meaning went when operations moved to the MCP server. The
# old name is still honoured, with a warning.
SVPCHAIN_AGENT_CHAIN_ID="svp-2517-1"

# The remote MCP server this agent's operations run on (svpchain-dex-mcp). It
# serves MCP at the root of its listener, so this is an origin and not a path.
#
# Unlike the chain endpoints above this is not loopback by default: there is a
# deployed server already serving the catalog, and pointing at it is what
# works out of the box. To run svpchain-dex-mcp beside this agent instead,
# set this to its port — network_mode: host means 127.0.0.1 reaches it.
SVPCHAIN_MCP_ENDPOINT="https://dex-mcp-testnet.svpchain.org/"

# Enables the svpchain-assistant skill, which answers a plain-English question
# by planning MCP tool calls. Unset, the skill is not served and the card does
# not advertise it — so turning it on or off moves the card, and the deployment
# needs a --register run afterwards like any other card change.
#
# This is the one secret shipped to the deployed host: the agent calls the model
# at runtime, so the key lands there as assistant.env at 0600, referenced by the
# compose env_file. It is never written into agent.toml.
#
# Left COMMENTED OUT for the same reason as the owner key below: this file is
# sourced, so an uncommented line reading a secret you have not stored yet would
# fail the source and take every command down with it.
#SVPCHAIN_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="$(op read op://vault/anthropic/api-key)"

# To run the planner on the openai chat-completions format instead — OpenAI
# itself, DeepSeek, or a local runtime — set the provider, the endpoint and the
# model, and supply the matching key. The endpoint and model are ordinary
# settings; only the key is a secret.
#SVPCHAIN_ASSISTANT_PROVIDER="openai"
#SVPCHAIN_ASSISTANT_BASE_URL="https://api.deepseek.com"
#SVPCHAIN_ASSISTANT_MODEL="deepseek-v4-pro"
#SVPCHAIN_OPENAI_API_KEY="$(op read op://vault/deepseek/api-key)"

# The URL callers reach this agent at. Used verbatim — nothing is appended, so
# it must be the URL your reverse proxy routes to this agent.
SVPCHAIN_PERPS_AGENT_PUBLIC_URL="https://perps-agent.svpchain.org"

# ---- registration ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The OWNER key: the account that registers this agent on chain, pays the
# registration fee and the bond, and controls the record thereafter. Needed
# only by --register. It is never shipped to the deployed host — the agent
# signs nothing, so a key there would be risk with no use.
#
# This one account is registered as both owner and operator, so the agent id
# (did:svp:<address>) derives from it and it can register exactly ONE agent.
# A second agent needs its own key, in its own config directory.
#
# ./scripts/deploy.sh --gen-owner-key mints one and rewrites the line below.
# Setting it by hand is fine too; this file is sourced, so it can compute the
# value rather than store it:
#   SVPCHAIN_PERPS_AGENT_OWNER_KEY="$(op read op://vault/svpchain-perps-agent/owner-key)"
#
# Left COMMENTED OUT deliberately. This file is sourced, so an uncommented line
# reading a key you have not created yet would fail the source itself, and
# every command would then die on that instead of running keyless as intended.
#SVPCHAIN_PERPS_AGENT_OWNER_KEY="$(cat "$HOME/.config/svpchain-perps-agent/owner.key")"

# The registry chain's Cosmos REST API (the gRPC-gateway, typically :1317), as
# reachable from THIS machine. Required by --register: the transaction is
# signed and broadcast here, not on the deploy host. When only the deploy host
# can see the node, tunnel it first and point this at the local end:
#   ssh -N -L 1317:127.0.0.1:1317 <deploy host>
#
# There was a gRPC route beside this one. It only ever suited a local dev node,
# and left unset it turned "you have not said how to reach the chain" into a
# dial timeout against localhost.
#SVPCHAIN_AGENT_CHAIN_REST="http://127.0.0.1:1317"

# Capability tags the registration is indexed by, and free-form metadata.
SVPCHAIN_OPERATOR_CAPABILITIES="perps.trading,perps.market-data"
SVPCHAIN_OPERATOR_METADATA="svpchain perps agent"

# Price advertised on chain: an integer in the settlement token's smallest unit
# per unit of work (1000000 is one USDV at six decimals, per call). It is
# informational for discovery; fee collection happens in the settlement contract.
SVPCHAIN_OPERATOR_PRICE_AMOUNT="1000000"
SVPCHAIN_OPERATOR_PRICE_UNIT="call"

# ---- install location ------------------------------------------------------

# Where the agent is installed on the remote host.
#SVPCHAIN_INSTALL_DIR="~/svpchain-perps-agent"
